The purpose of this research paper is to derive optimal estimates for the first eigenvalue of the first Robin eigenvalue problem involving the anisotropic $p$-Laplace operator. This problem seeks to find the first eigenfunction and corresponding eigenvalue for the given operator. Our research compares the eigenvalue to two distinct functionals, and by doing so, yields a precise upper bound for the eigenvalue in question. This method also allows us to extend the obtained estimates to more general nonlinear elliptic operators. Furthermore, we investigate the behavior of the eigenvalue as $p$ approaches either $1$ or $\infty$. Lastly, this study can be applied to derive Caccioppi estimates in the context of $p$-harmonic functions. Additionally, we discuss the implications of our results on Q-values and quasicontinuity in the case where $p>1$. Lastly, we examine the particular instance of $p=2$ and derive a novel estimate for the first eigenvalue under this condition. This research can potentially be applied to various problems in geometric analysis and PDEs, and may provide valuable insights in the future. 
